WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.723
Passwords are authentication secrets.

2
00:00:02.723 --> 00:00:08.490
They are not ordinary form fields, comments, profile preferences, or support notes.

3
00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:16.499
A password can unlock account access, user data, administrative actions, billing workflows, and connected systems.

4
00:00:16.499 --> 00:00:22.426
That is why password handling needs explicit design across the full account lifecycle.

5
00:00:22.426 --> 00:00:25.149
Secure handling starts before storage.

6
00:00:25.149 --> 00:00:41.168
It includes collection in the user interface, protected transport, server-side validation, hashing, verification, reset, account recovery, logging, storage, migration, monitoring, support procedures, and compromise response.

7
00:00:41.168 --> 00:00:48.376
A weak choice in any one of those areas can expose password material or make account abuse easier to sustain.

8
00:00:48.376 --> 00:00:50.619
The defensive goal has two sides.

9
00:00:50.619 --> 00:01:01.031
First, reduce online account abuse by making registration, login, reset, and recovery flows predictable, monitored, and resistant to repeated misuse.

10
00:01:01.031 --> 00:01:08.400
Second, reduce offline damage if a database, backup, log source, crash dump, or analytics stream is exposed.

11
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.526
Correct password hashing is important, but it is only one part of the system.

12
00:01:13.526 --> 00:01:24.979
A password should not be stored, logged, displayed, transmitted, copied into tickets, or repeated back to a user unless there is a clearly justified and secure workflow.

13
00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:28.583
Password security depends on application code, ASP.

14
00:01:28.583 --> 00:01:37.714
NET Core Identity configuration, infrastructure, database access, key management, operational procedures, and support behavior.

15
00:01:37.714 --> 00:01:43.400
Treating passwords as high-value secrets keeps the review practical and grounded.
